A||N||5||WhiteFieroBlur||Need help with my new fiero||||1||
Z||000000||WhiteFieroBlur||05-20-2000||12:41 PM||||I bought my White 1987 GT Fiero a couple of months ago and I would like to know what things to do to it first. The guy before me had it sitting for a while. I changed the oil and just changed the fuel filter and I have had to buy 2 fan belts already cause they broke off. I put on one that has lasted longer than the others but I still hear it squeaking and its starting to wear out. Someone said its due to my pullys being a little off. My fuel gage only shows a quarter tank when it gets full. Anyone know if I have to completely change it or if its just wires that needs looking at. It will go down but only shows a quarter tank when full. I don't want to run outta gas  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>  <BR>  Also before I bought it, the person before me had an engine overhaul done on it and with the engine running on idle, I can hear it going up and down in sound and when the fan kicks in or I turn on the A/C the battery really pulls down and the car sounds like its gonna cut off on me. And my catalytic converter at the bottom needs replacing cause its rattling pretty bad. I am starting to think maybe I should have gotten another car, but I know that when I get my car fixed up nice that it will all be worth it.<P>  But I love this car, I live in a small town and I think I am the only person living here who has a GT model and everyone is always wanting to race me when I cruiz around cause this is a mustang and camaro town. Everyone has one and me being new to a standard, I tend to get left in the dust  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> If anyone has any question or suggestions on getting my car running smooth again let me know. Thanx||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phaeton||05-20-2000||03:32 PM||||The waterpump or alternator bearings are dragging.  Spin them by hand with the belt off, should spin freely.<P>Bet the fuel sender arm is sticking also if the Fiero sat for awhile with an empty tank.<P>Prolonged downtime is rough on everything, cables, return springs, electric connections, calipers, etc.<P>Dust settles in, oils drain down, metals corrode together, greases gel, adhesives dry out.  Temporary high maintenance that goes away as each problem is searched out and corrected.<P>Owning a Fiero that runs is never a mistake, although the craving for more grows ever stronger, more power,more speed, more shine, more Fiero's.<P>Advice from the forum on all of this is given, be specific and keep a sense of humor.  ||||reg||12
Z||000002||WhiteFieroBlur||05-20-2000||06:22 PM||||  That was my first post and I don't really want another car. I wash it almost everyday and I just spent hours in the hot sun cleaning the inside and out just to go cruizing around in it later. Most people around here don't have a clue what kind of car it is, and you know that look on thier face when you tell them the engine is in the back.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>   But I just want to make sure I have my fiero engine in good shape and right now it doesn't sound right. I'll try and be more specific and have more details with my posts.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||05-20-2000||11:11 PM||||First check all rubber hoses and Vacuum lines. The vacuum lines may have a bad one causing your unstable idle. Next buy the STP extra strength Injector cleaner. Actually buy 2 bottles and use them on 2 consecutive fill-ups. This should free up the fuel sending unit. (mine was stuck at full, now it works normal) Remove belts and check water pump and alternator for bad bearings. The easiest way to hear which one is bad is use a long screw driver put the blade near the shaft of either and spin by hand slowly then fast. If one doesn't spin freely that one is bad. If they spin freely, listen for the one that sounds like "sand" is in it. Some bearings will be worse under a load but when you remove belt tension they may spin fine. All electrical connectors need to be checked. You can clean them with an old toothbrush and T.V. tuner cleaner (available at most electrical stores). Since it sat for a while I would think about pulling the distributor and replacing the "O" ring and since the distributor is out, replace the pick-up coil. Do a regular tune-up (plugs,cap,rotor,wires) Check that the coolant looks good. You may want to have a flush done on it if it looks brown.||||reg||1
